+# SPEC: CSV import of contacts
+
+## Why
+
+Sales keeps leads in spreadsheets. Adding contacts one at a time is too slow, so they
+want to load a whole CSV file into contactbook in one go. They also need to be told
+exactly what happened: which contacts were created, which were updated, and which rows
+were rejected and why, so they can fix the spreadsheet and import again.
+
+The file uses the same columns as our CSV export (`export.py`: `name`, `email`,
+`phone`, `tags`, with tags joined by `;`).
+
+## Interface (fixed; other code calls exactly this)
+
+- `src/contactbook/importer.py`: `import_contacts(repo: InMemoryContactRepository, data: str | bytes) -> ImportReport`
+  where `data` is the file content: raw bytes as read from disk, or already-decoded text.
+- `ImportReport` (dataclass, same module): `created: list[str]`, `updated: list[str]` (normalised
+  emails), `errors: list[RowIssue]`, `warnings: list[RowIssue]`.
+- `RowIssue` (dataclass, same module): `row: int`, `column: str | None`, `message: str`.
+- `ImportFileError(ContactError)` in `src/contactbook/errors.py`, raised when the file as a whole
+  can't be imported.
+
+## Terms
+
+- **Row number**: the row number Sales sees when they open the file in Excel. The
+  header is row 1 and the first line after it is row 2. Every line counts, blank lines
+  included.
+- **Blank row**: an empty line, or a row where every cell is empty or contains only
+  whitespace (e.g. Excel's `,,,` lines).
+- **Contact row**: any data row that is not blank, whether valid or not.
+- **Applied row**: a contact row that created or changed a contact.
+
+## Acceptance criteria
+
+### Whole-file outcome
+
+1. A file is either rejected as a whole, by raising `ImportFileError`, or processed row
+   by row and an `ImportReport` returned. `ImportFileError` is a subclass of
+   `ContactError`.
+2. When `ImportFileError` is raised, the repository is exactly as it was before the
+   call. No contact is added or changed.
+3. The whole file is rejected (`ImportFileError`) when:
+   - it is completely empty, or has no header line;
+   - the header has no `email` column or no `name` column;
+   - the same column appears more than once in the header (after the header matching
+     rules in 8);
+   - `data` is bytes that are not valid UTF-8. The message tells the user to re-save
+     the file as "CSV UTF-8";
+   - the file has more than 1000 contact rows. Blank rows don't count towards the
+     limit, so a file with exactly 1000 contact rows is accepted.
+4. If the header is missing `email` or `name` and the header line contains `;`, the
+   error message hints that the file looks semicolon-separated and must be
+   comma-separated.
+5. A file with a valid header and no contact rows (only the header, or the header
+   followed only by blank rows) is not an error. It returns an empty report and
+   leaves the repository unchanged.
+
+### Encoding and format
+
+6. Bytes input is decoded as UTF-8. A leading UTF-8 BOM is accepted and ignored, both
+   in bytes input and in text input that still starts with the BOM character
+   (`﻿`). It never becomes part of the first column name.
+7. The delimiter is a comma. Standard CSV quoting is supported, including quoted cells
+   that contain commas, quotes or line breaks.
+8. Header names are matched case-insensitively and ignoring surrounding whitespace, and
+   columns may come in any order (`" Email"`, `NAME`, `Tags`).
+9. `phone` and `tags` columns are optional. Leaving either one out is not an error or
+   a warning.
+10. Unknown columns (e.g. `Company`) are never an error. Their values are ignored, and
+    each one produces one warning with `row` 1 and `column` set to the header text as
+    written in the file.
+
+### Row processing
+
+11. Blank rows are skipped silently, with no error and no warning, but they still take
+    up a row number.
+12. Every contact row produces exactly one of: a new contact (its email in `created`),
+    a change to an existing contact (its email in `updated`), one or more entries in
+    `errors`, or nothing if it matches the stored contact exactly.
+13. Name, email, phone and tags are validated and normalised by the existing rules
+    (`validation.py` / `Contact.create`), the same as for a contact added by hand. For
+    example, `" Ann.Lee@Example.COM "` is stored and reported as
+    `ann.lee@example.com`.
+14. A row that fails validation is not applied at all, not even partly to an existing
+    contact. It gets at least one entry in `errors` with that row's row number. If the
+    problem belongs to one field, `column` is that field's lower-case name (`name`,
+    `email`, `phone` or `tags`). The message is human-readable. Reporting only the
+    first problem in a row, or all of them, are both acceptable.
+15. An empty or missing `name` or `email` cell is a row error on that column.
+16. The tags cell is split on `;`, and each piece is normalised with the existing tag
+    helper. Empty pieces (`vip;;lead;`) are ignored and repeats collapse. If any tag is
+    invalid, the whole row is an error with `column` = `tags` and the row isn't
+    applied.
+17. A row with fewer cells than the header treats the missing cells as empty. A row
+    with more cells than the header is an error (`column` may be `None`) if any of the
+    extra cells is non-empty. Extra cells that are empty are ignored.
+18. Valid rows are applied even when other rows in the same file have errors.
+
+### New and existing contacts
+
+19. A valid row whose email is not in the repository creates a contact. An empty phone
+    cell, or no phone column, means the new contact has no phone.
+20. A valid row whose email is already in the repository (compared after normalisation)
+    updates that contact. It never creates a second contact and is never an error. On
+    update:
+    - **name** is replaced by the file's value;
+    - **phone** is replaced only when the cell has a value. An empty cell, or no phone
+      column, keeps the stored phone;
+    - **tags** from the file are added to the stored tags. An import never removes a
+      tag.
+21. An updated contact's email appears in `updated` only if something actually
+    changed. A row that matches the stored contact exactly appears in neither list and
+    produces no error.
+
+### Duplicate emails within one file
+
+22. Emails are compared after normalisation (`Ann@X.com` = `ann@x.com`).
+23. The first applied row for an email wins. Every later row with that email is an
+    error on the later row (`column` = `email`), isn't applied, and its message names
+    the row number of the row that won.
+24. A row with errors doesn't claim its email. If row 3 has `ann@x.com` with a bad
+    phone and row 7 has `ann@x.com` and is valid, row 7 is applied.
+25. As a result, no email appears more than once across `created` and `updated`
+    combined.
+
+### Report
+
+26. `created` and `updated` hold normalised emails in file order. `errors` and
+    `warnings` are in row order.
+27. Round trip: importing the output of `export_csv(repo.all())` into an empty
+    repository produces the same contacts (name, email, phone, tags), all listed in
+    `created`, with no errors and no warnings.
+28. Importing the same file twice in a row: the second import has empty `created`,
+    `updated` and `errors`.
+
+## Non-goals
+
+- Encodings other than UTF-8 (cp1252, UTF-16, …), and auto-detecting the encoding.
+- Delimiters other than comma, and delimiter sniffing.
+- Files without a header row, or mapping differently named columns (e.g. "E-mail
+  address") to our fields.
+- Removing tags, clearing phones or deleting contacts through an import.
+- Partial import of over-limit files. Above 1000 contact rows, the user splits the
+  file.
+- A dry-run or preview mode, a rollback of a successful import, streaming very large
+  files, or progress reporting.
+- Persistence adapters, the HTTP endpoint and any UI. Those live in other packages and
+  call `import_contacts`.
+- Exact row numbers for rows that come after a quoted cell spanning several lines.
+  Any consistent numbering is acceptable there.
+- Wrapping programming errors. Passing something other than `str`/`bytes` as `data`
+  may raise `TypeError` as usual (see `errors.py`).
